From 2a46cf5e34d1b57c3f534a977c5a1f0db1e19d59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:57:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(parser): answer the closer probe from a forward window,
 not a hash table
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`has_closer_from` asked whether a closing byte follows an opener by hashing
`(slice pointer, slice length, closer)` into a lazily allocated table holding
the position of that slice's last closer, with a one-entry cell in front of
it. Every `[` of a paragraph, every `![`, every `[^`, every `<` and every `{`
pays that hash, and link-dense prose asks it once per bracket.

The question is monotone in the position asked about — a closer at or after
`from` is one at or after every earlier position too — so a window answers it
instead: a range of bytes known to hold no such closer, and the closer just
behind that range. A question inside the window is a comparison; one past it
resumes the `memchr` where the window ends, and one behind it reads only up
to the window's start, because from there on the window already decides. The
run of unclosed openers the table was introduced for therefore still reads
every byte once, by the first opener that reaches it, and the parser carries
three words per closer instead of a table pointer and a cached key.

The window is kept in addresses rather than in offsets of one named slice.
Nested content is parsed as slices of one buffer, and "no `]` lies between
these two addresses" holds for every slice that covers them, so a window
built for a paragraph still answers inside a bracket text cut out of it and
one built for the whole source still answers inside the paragraphs of it.
One window per closer keeps `[`, `<` and `{` from evicting each other, which
is what would have turned their interleaving back into a walk per opener.

The answer is unchanged for every input. Both forms compute "is there a
`closer` at or after `from` in this slice", the window only from bytes it has
read: a hit is a byte the scan just passed and is reported only while it lies
before the slice end, and a miss is reported only for a range the scan
covered to that end. A position past the slice is `false` either way, as the
old `memrchr` position could never reach it. A differential corpus of 120,252
bracket-, angle- and brace-heavy sources — token soup, narrow bracket soup
and the hand-picked run shapes — rendered under five option sets (CommonMark,
GFM, MDX, all extensions, all extensions at a cap of 4) is byte-identical in
HTML and in the AST `Debug` against the previous commit across all 601,260
renders. `cargo test --workspace --all-features --locked` passes with no
`.snap.new` file, so the snapshot suites and the CommonMark 0.31.2 and GFM
conformance baselines are unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
